store's processes before a build that changes it. Every step decides by its stamp (``target/NAME.stamp``, the pin of the sources it was built from: a submodule's commit, `native/probe.c`'s git hash), never by its output existing, and each step calls the ones it needs: after a sync to a new Jolt pin, `bin/build seon` rebuilds Jolt first (a target/jolt left from the old pin made every root-booting test fail on a var the new source needs), and with no pin moved every step says \"up to date\". `hello` and `seon` are stamped by the tree (`bin/tree`: HEAD's tree plus every changed and untracked file) and the chez and jolt pins, so an unchanged tree relinks neither (0.6 s, was 32 s) and any edit rebuilds both." "docs/checks.md#modes 67%: A step is a mode of `target/seon` (except `laws`, which is `bin/laws`, `lmdb-kill` and `lmdb-sem`, native programs on LMDB envs of their own, and `forks`, `bin/forks`: the forks' own suites on Jolt, [forks](forks.md#own-suites)), its body over one harness that boots root, runs it, and ends on the one verdict line `NAME OK` or `NAME FAILED` with its exit status (`seon.check/run-check`, [machine](machine.md#builds-and-checks)). The harness is a driver, not the program: `seon.check` lives in `test/seon/check.clj`, outside src's count (task `seon.main/drivers-leave-src`). The binary's main is ```seon.main/-main```: it serves `root` and `ports` itself and, for every other mode, loads the driver from the checkout's source at run time (its deps.edn roots added to the loader, `test/` and `src/` to the source roots), so the release binary holds only the program (220.4 MB, against 238.1 MB with the driver compiled in) and a check process starts about 0.7 s later (1.7 s for `steps`, cached, against 1.0 s). `bin/check` runs no binary built from another tree: `bin/build` records the tree it built from (`target/seon.tree`, named by `bin/tree`, as ```seon.fx/tree-id``` names it), and a run or a mode whose checkout is another tree is refused, naming both, with `MODE FAILED (stale binary)` (`seon.check-test/a-stale-binary-is-refused`). The tree is the whole checkout's less the paths `:seon.config.source/tree-excludes` names (`notes/`: working papers no read and no build reads, so a merge-log line moves no digest and rebuilds nothing), so any other edit, a wiki page included, asks for `bin/build seon` before the next single mode; `bin/prove` builds first whenever a step it selects runs the binary, so its runs are never refused. A worktree starts with main's binary copied, which refused a worktree's config whose keys differed before it said anything. A mode's seed and drop are quiet when they work; when one fails, its own output and `MODE FAILED (seed)` (or `(drop)`) are printed (`seon.check-test/a-failed-seed-says-failed`): no step ends without its verdict line."], :functions ["seon.main/-main 54%: The binary's modes: `root` (root-serve) and `ports` here, every other one the check driver's (driver!), with the checkout's root the parent of the working directory (bin/check and bin/root run the binary in target/)." "seon.main/driver! 46%: The check driver's -main (seon.check, in test/: task seon.main/drivers-leave-src), loaded now from the checkout's source (checkout-roots!), so the release binary holds only the program and a check run compiles the driver it runs."], :schemas [[:seon.config.agent/test-ticks "57%"] [:seon.config.source/gate-checkout "42%"]]}}
