message" "docs/render.md#tailwind" "seon.render.ui/page" "seon.render.ui/root-input"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/render.md#tailwind 70%: The page is laid out by Tailwind's classes in each render's hiccup, responsive at every width, with no hand-written stylesheet (owner, 2026-10-23: \"I want responsive html and css so why not tailwind?\"). `resources/public/css/input.css` holds every directive, token and source glob (Tailwind v4, CSS-first, no config file): `source(none)` with an explicit `@source` over `src/**/*.clj` (automatic detection walked the reference-code tree: v1 measured 42 s against 1 s), the Phosphor theme's tokens (dark, light when the system asks), a base layer for semantic hiccup and the one printer's classes, and a safelist of the utilities an agent may use, since a render an agent defines at its REPL is in no scanned file: the common layout, spacing, type, list, border and theme-color utilities, responsive where layout is (117 KB, 16 KB gzipped; a rebuild on each agent render's classes was the alternative, priced and not taken). `bin/build css` (a step of `bin/build seon`, and so of the `:build` effect after merges) runs Tailwind's standalone binary, pinned by version and checksum and fetched once into `target/tailwind/`, into `target/css/app.css` (`:seon.config.web/stylesheet`, served at `/app.css` and read per request); its stamp is the digest of `input.css` and the scanned sources, so unchanged inputs regenerate nothing (68 ms when they changed, v1's port of its `bin/css`). Add a class to a render, or a token or safelist entry to `input.css`, and build: the stylesheet regenerates." "docs/render.md#a-value-on-the-page-is-its-structure 66%: The page draws a value from its data, the model reads it as text, and both come from one selection: `seon.print.emit`'s hiccup fold reads the same operation stream the layout reads, so the text is unchanged by what the page shows (owner, 2026-10-25: \"We can do more complex representations of values there\"). A map is a grid of key and value rows, folded as any collection is (a map of scalars, or the value printed, not at all). Two or more maps in a vector sharing two or more keys are a `<table>`, a column per key and a row per map (`seon.print.emit/table`). Text marked as itself is drawn as itself: code, `{:seon.print/code s}` (`seon.print/code`), verbatim in `<pre><code>` and highlighted as Clojure by highlight.js (served at `/highlight.js` from `:seon.config.web/public-dir`): one observer in the page's shell (`seon.render.ui/shell`) highlights each `code.language-clojure` (`seon.print.emit/highlight`) as it arrives and again when a patch morphs a row back to its plain text, so a per-element init, which a morph of unchanged attributes never re-runs, is not used; each mutation batch costs one scan of the page's code elements, and a screen eval opened under its turn carries the same `eval-eN` anchor; prose, `{:seon.print/prose s}`, a paragraph; both print verbatim and unquoted, and a cut of either reads its rest through its key, `(subs (:seon.print/prose (get-in result/e3 [:plan])) 1200)`. A page an agent built is the value's own `:seon.render/html` key, a render already made, and renders as that hiccup wherever the value sits (`seon.render/nested`). A handle, `result/e12`, links to its eval's row, whose id is `eval-e12`, open or not. In the conversation an eval is one collapsed line (`.seon-eval`, ```seon.render.ui/eval-item```, the 2026-10-24 mockup's `▸ (form) · 12 ms`): the prose beside it, its code's first line, its time and its handle, opening to the eval's own render (```seon.render.ui/evaluation```: its code and its value); the agents are a column beside it on a wide screen and a strip of chips above it on a narrow one; prose (a model's reply, a message) reads in the system's text face (`--font-prose`), code and data in mono. A cut opens where it stands: its marker is a button whose click posts `/open` with its row's id and the form the cut prints, `(drop 1 (get-in result/e157 [:value :proofs]))`, and the row renders what that form reads from the record (`seon.print/read-rest`, the operators continuations are made of), so the page shows a cut's rest as the model would read it, under that form as the button that closes it (a button, not a `<details>`: the row's re-render would close a details, whose toggle would post the close); the form is read once, at the edge (`seon.web/open-form`), and a row renders again only when its set of opened cuts changes (its component key). An evaluation's row renders its response's value this way, its cost and the functions it called beside it; a value not held as data (`:printed`) shows as it was printed. `seon.print-test/a-map-is-key-and-value-rows`, `maps-sharing-keys-are-a-table`, `code-and-prose-read-as-themselves`; `seon.render-test/an-eval-row-renders-its-value-as-structure`, `a-page-an-agent-built-renders-nested`."], :functions [["seon.render.ui/evaluation 54%: One eval (the declared html render of :seon.db/eval-fact, its record, and of a response), as it ran: the prose beside it, its code, its response printed under its handle as it is shown (seon.print/record-text of its record; a live response's own :text; an error's by its error map's render; a value recorded as its basis, @ctx's, rendered from the branch at it), and its cost and who evaluated it as recorded; :as :inline, its code and response in one line." [:seon.eval/by :seon.eval/record :seon.render/opts]] "seon.render.ui/page-chrome 52%: A page in root's chrome (root's, an agent's): the top bar, then the units its walks drew, each placed in the list it was drawn in ({:id :container :rev :html}: the agents list, the head, the task's list, the conversation, the tiles), and its caption and input." ["seon.render.ui/debug 50%: The debug page of id (an agent's id, :root) laid out: what its model reads." [:seon.render/page-more]] ["seon.render.ui/eval-item 46%: An eval of the conversation (a component over ctx), as a reference, never a 